# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = '1 Peter'
TESTAMENT = 'New Testament'
GENRE = 'letters'
SUBTITLE = 'A Living Hope, a Holy Walk, and Grace in the Trial of Faith'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', '1 Peter is a letter from “Peter, an apostle of Jesus Christ, to the strangers scattered throughout Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia” (1 Pet. 1:1). It speaks about a lively hope, an inheritance reserved in heaven, the trial of faith, holy conduct, submission, suffering for well doing, and the care of the elders for the flock. Peter says he has written briefly, “exhorting, and testifying” (1 Pet. 5:12).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', '1 Peter is addressed to “the strangers scattered throughout Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia” (1 Pet. 1:1). It is read on that audience marker and on the letter’s own setting, in which its readers are called “a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people” (1 Pet. 2:9). This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting.')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of 1 Peter', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the letter — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — A Living Hope and a Holy Walk (1:1–2:10)'), ('p', 'Peter blesses God for a lively hope and an inheritance reserved in heaven, speaks of the trial of faith, calls for holiness and fervent love, and turns to the living stone and the chosen generation (1 Pet. 1:3–9, 13–16, 22; 2:4–10).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Conduct and Submission (2:11–3:12)'), ('p', 'Peter urges abstaining from fleshly lusts and honest conversation among the Gentiles, submission to every ordinance of man, patience in suffering after the example of Christ, and right conduct for wives, husbands, and all (1 Pet. 2:11–3:12).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Suffering for Righteousness (3:13–4:19)'), ('p', 'Peter writes of suffering for righteousness’ sake, of Christ who suffered once for sins, of arming with the same mind, of the fiery trial, and of judgment that must begin at the house of God (1 Pet. 3:13–18; 4:1–19).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Elders, Humility, and the Adversary (Ch. 5)'), ('p', 'Peter exhorts the elders to feed the flock, speaks of the chief Shepherd, calls all to humility and watchfulness against the adversary, and closes with greetings (1 Pet. 5:1–14).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in 1 Peter', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how 1 Peter should be understood within Israel’s prophetic program. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants readers to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>A Living Hope and an Inheritance</b> — Peter writes of a lively hope and of “an inheritance incorruptible, and undefiled, and that fadeth not away, reserved in heaven for you” (1 Pet. 1:3–4).', '<b>The Trial of Faith</b> — The trial of faith is “much more precious than of gold that perisheth” (1 Pet. 1:7).', '<b>Holy Conduct</b> — “Be ye holy; for I am holy” (1 Pet. 1:16).', '<b>A Chosen People</b> — The readers “in time past were not a people, but are now the people of God” (1 Pet. 2:10).', '<b>Suffering and Glory</b> — The readers are partakers of Christ’s sufferings, “when his glory shall be revealed” (1 Pet. 4:13).', '<b>Humility and Care</b> — “Casting all your care upon him; for he careth for you” (1 Pet. 5:7).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in 1 Peter', [('p', 'This section gathers what the letter teaches about Christ — who He is, what He has done, and how He is presented. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered focus before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these verses together, you get a clear picture of how 1 Peter presents Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Risen Lord</b> — The readers are begotten again “by the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead” (1 Pet. 1:3).', '<b>The Precious Blood</b> — They were redeemed with “the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot” (1 Pet. 1:19).', '<b>The Living Stone</b> — Christ is “a living stone, disallowed indeed of men, but chosen of God, and precious” (1 Pet. 2:4).', '<b>The Suffering Christ</b> — “Christ also suffered for us, leaving us an example,” and “his own self bare our sins in his own body on the tree” (1 Pet. 2:21, 24).', '<b>At the Right Hand of God</b> — He “is gone into heaven, and is on the right hand of God” (1 Pet. 3:22).', '<b>The Chief Shepherd</b> — “When the chief Shepherd shall appear, ye shall receive a crown of glory that fadeth not away” (1 Pet. 5:4).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in 1 Peter', [('p', 'This section helps you see how 1 Peter speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. 1 Peter operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read 1 Peter in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'The letter’s audience markers are read as written:'), ('ul', ['Peter writes “to the strangers scattered throughout Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia” (1 Pet. 1:1).', 'The readers were redeemed from a “vain conversation received by tradition from your fathers” (1 Pet. 1:18).', 'They are called “a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people” (1 Pet. 2:9), who “in time past were not a people, but are now the people of God” (1 Pet. 2:10).', 'The letter speaks of the Gentiles among whom the readers have their conversation (1 Pet. 2:12) and whose will they once wrought (1 Pet. 4:3).', 'Sara is named, and the readers are told “whose daughters ye are” (1 Pet. 3:6).', 'The letter does not use the name the Body of Christ for its readers.']), ('p', 'This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in 1 Peter that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how 1 Peter contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Audience — The letter is addressed to the strangers scattered throughout Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia (1 Pet. 1:1).', 'A Lively Hope — Begotten again to a lively hope and to an inheritance reserved in heaven (1 Pet. 1:3–4).', 'Holiness — “Be ye holy; for I am holy.” (1 Pet. 1:15–16).', 'A Chosen People — “a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people.” (1 Pet. 2:9–10).', 'Submission — “Submit yourselves to every ordinance of man for the Lord’s sake.” (1 Pet. 2:13–17).', 'Judgment Begins — The time is come that “judgment must begin at the house of God.” (1 Pet. 4:17).', 'The Elders — “Feed the flock of God” and the chief Shepherd will give a crown of glory (1 Pet. 5:1–4).', 'The Adversary — “your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour.” (1 Pet. 5:8–9).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What 1 Peter Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in 1 Peter and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on the prophetic setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what 1 Peter teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('p', 'Program Identity shows where a truth belongs. “Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth” marks a statement, command, warning or instruction addressed to Israel within its covenant and kingdom setting; it is not addressed to the Body of Christ and does not transfer to it. A verse that names Israel as the audience is labeled the same way, because it fixes the program identity of the letter.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in 1 Peter', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Audience', 'The letter is addressed to the strangers scattered throughout Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia.', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Pet. 1:1'], ['A Lively Hope', 'Begotten again to a lively hope and to an inheritance reserved in heaven.', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Pet. 1:3–4'], ['Holiness', '“Be ye holy; for I am holy.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Pet. 1:15–16'], ['A Chosen People', '“a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, an holy nation, a peculiar people.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Pet. 2:9–10'], ['Submission', '“Submit yourselves to every ordinance of man for the Lord’s sake.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Pet. 2:13–17'], ['Judgment Begins', 'The time is come that “judgment must begin at the house of God.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Pet. 4:17'], ['The Elders', '“Feed the flock of God” and the chief Shepherd will give a crown of glory.', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Pet. 5:1–4'], ['The Adversary', '“your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', '1 Pet. 5:8–9']], [18, 40, 24, 18])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in 1 Peter that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what 1 Peter teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['God Judges Without Partiality', 'The Father “without respect of persons judgeth according to every man’s work.”', '1 Pet. 1:17'], ['The Word Endures', '“the word of the Lord endureth for ever.”', '1 Pet. 1:25'], ['Honour All Men', '“Honour all men.”', '1 Pet. 2:17'], ['Seek Peace', '“let him seek peace, and ensue it.”', '1 Pet. 3:11'], ['The Eyes of the Lord', '“the eyes of the Lord are over the righteous.”', '1 Pet. 3:12'], ['Love Covers', '“charity shall cover the multitude of sins.”', '1 Pet. 4:8'], ['A Faithful Creator', 'Those who suffer according to the will of God commit their souls “as unto a faithful Creator.”', '1 Pet. 4:19'], ['Humility and Grace', '“God resisteth the proud, and giveth grace to the humble.”', '1 Pet. 5:5']], [28, 50, 22])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of 1 Peter without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s prophetic story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing Israel-program commands for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to 1 Peter in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Rest in fair judgment</b> — God judges without respect of persons (1 Pet. 1:17).', '<b>Stand on the word</b> — The word of the Lord endures for ever (1 Pet. 1:25).', '<b>Honour all men</b> — Give honour to every person (1 Pet. 2:17).', '<b>Seek peace</b> — Pursue it and keep after it (1 Pet. 3:11).', '<b>Live before the Lord</b> — His eyes are over the righteous (1 Pet. 3:12).', '<b>Love fervently</b> — Love covers a multitude of sins (1 Pet. 4:8).', '<b>Commit your soul</b> — Keep doing well, trusting a faithful Creator (1 Pet. 4:19).', '<b>Humble yourself</b> — God gives grace to the humble (1 Pet. 5:5).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for 1 Peter', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach 1 Peter in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['A Living Hope (1:1–12) — Inheritance and the trial of faith. <i>(1 Pet. 1:3–9)</i>', 'A Holy Walk (1:13–2:3) — Holiness, redemption, and fervent love. <i>(1 Pet. 1:13–25)</i>', 'A Chosen Generation (2:4–10) — The living stone and the royal priesthood. <i>(1 Pet. 2:4–10)</i>', 'Conduct and Submission (2:11–3:12) — Among the Gentiles and in the home. <i>(1 Pet. 2:11–25; 3:1–12)</i>', 'Suffering for Righteousness (3:13–4:19) — The fiery trial and the house of God. <i>(1 Pet. 3:13–18; 4:12–19)</i>', 'Elders and Humility (5:1–11) — Feed the flock; resist the adversary. <i>(1 Pet. 5:1–10)</i>', 'Greetings (5:12–14) — Silvanus, and peace to all. <i>(1 Pet. 5:12–14)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read 1 Peter, helping you see both what the letter says and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications draw only on the universal truths 1 Peter itself states, so your response stays tied to the text.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching 1 Peter simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group see what the letter states and what it leaves unsaid, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, 1 Peter is read on its own audience marker, the scattered strangers of 1 Peter 1:1. This Companion shows you how to read the letter in its own setting while drawing out the universal truths it states.')])]

# Place in God's Storyline (Master Standard v1.155): last item of Section 1
from storyline import S as _STORY
SECTIONS[0][2].append(('p', '<b>Place in God’s Storyline.</b> ' + _STORY['1peter'][1]))
